WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.240 --> 00:00:03.169
INTRO:
Just one year ago, Robinhood was the up-and-coming

00:00:03.169 --> 00:00:07.329
mobile brokerage that millennials and retail
traders couldn’t get enough of.

00:00:07.329 --> 00:00:11.950
Robinhood’s total user count was surging
in the millions as users got addicted to Robinhood’s

00:00:11.950 --> 00:00:15.440
phenomenal UI and their gamification of investing.

00:00:15.440 --> 00:00:20.090
Ever since the GameStop fiasco and the collapse
of DogeCoin though, Robinhood has struggled

00:00:20.090 --> 00:00:23.509
to maintain the excitement or even maintain
a positive public opinion.

00:00:23.509 --> 00:00:28.750
In fact, so many people left terrible reviews
on the app store and the play store following

00:00:28.750 --> 00:00:34.250
the Gamestop situation that Robinhood’s
rating fell to 1 star at one point, and Google

00:00:34.250 --> 00:00:39.260
had to bail out Robinhood by removing reviews
that quote-on-quote violated Google’s policies.

00:00:39.260 --> 00:00:43.530
To make things worse, Robinhood has been consistently
losing hundreds of millions of dollars every

00:00:43.530 --> 00:00:46.680
single quarter forcing them to constantly
raise money.

00:00:46.680 --> 00:00:51.440
These worries were finally supposed to subside
with their IPO last year, but the IPO has

00:00:51.440 --> 00:00:56.220
only made things worse as it’s made it crystal
clear that the market doesn’t like Robinhood

00:00:56.220 --> 00:00:57.610
at all right now.

00:00:57.610 --> 00:01:02.300
Since their peak in August right after their
IPO, Robinhood stock has fallen 85% which

00:01:02.300 --> 00:01:07.740
correlates to a market cap loss of $56 billion
within just a matter of 6 months.

00:01:07.740 --> 00:01:11.890
This is actually super ironic given that their
current market cap is nearly equivalent to

00:01:11.890 --> 00:01:16.009
their cash reserves meaning that the market
is giving Robinhood’s business itself very

00:01:16.009 --> 00:01:17.009
little credit.

00:01:17.009 --> 00:01:22.280
So, what happened to Robinhood, and can they
recover from this nightmare.

00:01:22.280 --> 00:01:27.820
VIRTUOUS AMBITIONS:
Taking a look back, the story of Robinhood

00:01:27.820 --> 00:01:31.090
dates back to 2011 at the height of Occupy
Wall Street.

00:01:31.090 --> 00:01:35.100
If you’re familiar with this movement, it
was basically a protest against Wall Street

00:01:35.100 --> 00:01:37.399
bailouts following the 2008 financial recession.

00:01:37.399 --> 00:01:42.140
At the time, two college friends named Vlad
Tenev and Baiju Bhat had classic jobs on Wall

00:01:42.140 --> 00:01:43.140
Street.

00:01:43.140 --> 00:01:47.659
These guys were immigrants from Bulgaria and
India respectively and had met at Stanford.

00:01:47.659 --> 00:01:51.930
Initially, neither of them had any problems
or concerns with their careers.

00:01:51.930 --> 00:01:55.960
They were quickly moving up the corporate
ladder and were likely earning fat salaries,

00:01:55.960 --> 00:01:59.869
but all this changed during Occupy Wall Street
when one of their mutual friends accused them

00:01:59.869 --> 00:02:02.159
of profiteering from an unequal system.

00:02:02.159 --> 00:02:05.969
The duo didn’t instantly quit their jobs
or anything, but this statement stuck with

00:02:05.969 --> 00:02:09.220
them, and they started looking for ways in
which they could bridge the gap between retail

00:02:09.220 --> 00:02:10.970
investors and Wall Street.

00:02:10.970 --> 00:02:15.190
One of the most obvious disparities they stumbled
upon were the lofty fees and large minimum

00:02:15.190 --> 00:02:17.030
deposits required by most brokerages.

00:02:17.030 --> 00:02:21.580
Now, the fees weren’t actually that bad
in nominal terms given that it was just 60

00:02:21.580 --> 00:02:24.520
cents or a dollar per share or per option
that you purchased.

00:02:24.520 --> 00:02:29.080
But, from the backend, it only cost pennies
to execute each of these trades, so brokerages

00:02:29.080 --> 00:02:31.140
were enjoying massive profit margins.

00:02:31.140 --> 00:02:35.440
Combine this with the rise of free-to-use
games and apps like Clash of Clans and Facebook,

00:02:35.440 --> 00:02:39.380
and it was clear to Vlad and Bhaiju that they
could create a free-to-use brokerage.

00:02:39.380 --> 00:02:43.160
To match the underlying mission of the company,
they selected the name Robinhood which is

00:02:43.160 --> 00:02:46.880
of course a reference to the Disney character
that steals from the rich and gives to the

00:02:46.880 --> 00:02:47.880
poor.

00:02:47.880 --> 00:02:52.010
Aside from free trades and no minimum balances,
the duo placed a massive emphasis on the app’s

00:02:52.010 --> 00:02:53.010
UI.

00:02:53.010 --> 00:02:56.450
If the app was going to appeal to regular
people, it better be easy to navigate.

00:02:56.450 --> 00:03:00.510
Baiju says that his goal with the app design
was to make it quote “dead simple”.

00:03:00.510 --> 00:03:04.650
Every time he came up with a new feature or
design, he’d run over to Stanford from Silicon

00:03:04.650 --> 00:03:07.580
Valley to have random college students test
out the feature.

00:03:07.580 --> 00:03:11.530
Not only did this allow Baiju to develop an
intuitive app, but an app that was fun to

00:03:11.530 --> 00:03:12.530
use.

00:03:12.530 --> 00:03:16.660
You got confetti whenever you bought new stocks,
and Baiju paired this with clever vibrations

00:03:16.660 --> 00:03:19.290
and haptic feedback that kept the user engaged.

00:03:19.290 --> 00:03:23.970
Robinhood would later face charges for gamifying
investments, but this was great for appealing

00:03:23.970 --> 00:03:26.100
to and retaining new users.

00:03:26.100 --> 00:03:28.840
And with that, the duo was ready to launch
Robinhood.

00:03:28.840 --> 00:03:34.300
EXPLOSIVE GROWTH:
While Robinhood’s UI was phenomenal, the

00:03:34.300 --> 00:03:39.120
key to actually succeeding in the app market
is marketing whether that be organic or paid.

00:03:39.120 --> 00:03:43.710
Baiju and Vlad couldn’t pay for much marketing
due to their limited capital, so they decided

00:03:43.710 --> 00:03:46.730
to spark interest through another medium:
exclusivity.

00:03:46.730 --> 00:03:50.490
People who wanted to use Robinhood were placed
on a waiting list, and they could move up

00:03:50.490 --> 00:03:53.760
the waiting list by referring friends and
family to Robinhood.

00:03:53.760 --> 00:03:56.960
Not only did this make the original launch
easier to handle from the backend thanks to

00:03:56.960 --> 00:04:01.400
a limited user count, but it gave people who
made it through the cut exclusivity which

00:04:01.400 --> 00:04:02.910
is extremely powerful.

00:04:02.910 --> 00:04:07.600
Naturally, as humans, we crave exclusivity
because it gives us a sense of belonging and

00:04:07.600 --> 00:04:11.070
importance as well as a certain level of social
clout.

00:04:11.070 --> 00:04:15.530
Every luxury company out there leverages this
instinct to the max, but likely the best example

00:04:15.530 --> 00:04:17.689
of this within the tech world is Gmail.

00:04:17.689 --> 00:04:22.710
Back in 2004, when Gmail was launched, Google
was the small player going against players

00:04:22.710 --> 00:04:24.060
like Microsoft and Yahoo.

00:04:24.060 --> 00:04:29.360
Plus, they had limited infrastructure, so
they made Gmail invite-only, and this is how

00:04:29.360 --> 00:04:30.699
it was till 2007.

00:04:30.699 --> 00:04:35.599
Clearly, this worked out extremely well for
Gmail, and the same could be said about Robinhood.

00:04:35.599 --> 00:04:40.550
Following their initial launch in 2013, they
were able to build up a waiting list of 50,000

00:04:40.550 --> 00:04:44.220
people and by the time they launched on the
Apple app store, this number had ballooned

00:04:44.220 --> 00:04:45.979
to 1 million users.

00:04:45.979 --> 00:04:49.870
This massive user interest allowed Robinhood
to raise tens of millions from venture capitalists

00:04:49.870 --> 00:04:52.360
and start investing in new features and marketing.

00:04:52.360 --> 00:04:56.620
In 2018, for example, they started rolling
out the infamous Robinhood Crypto.

00:04:56.620 --> 00:04:59.360
And in late 2019, they launched fractional
shares.

00:04:59.360 --> 00:05:03.130
The new funding also allowed them to start
their famous free stock referral program that

00:05:03.130 --> 00:05:06.139
basically every finance YouTuber has milked
to the max.

00:05:06.139 --> 00:05:10.169
In the meantime, Robinhood was also upgrading
their backend infrastructure as well.

00:05:10.169 --> 00:05:14.060
In late 2018, for instance, they started to
self-clear their orders.

00:05:14.060 --> 00:05:18.030
All these new features made Robinhood more
and more established within the stock brokerage

00:05:18.030 --> 00:05:21.290
industry, and that’s when the pandemic hit
and the stock market crashed.

00:05:21.290 --> 00:05:25.749
Generally, this would be a terrible time for
brokerages, but a lot of retail traders were

00:05:25.749 --> 00:05:27.669
actually looking to buy the crash.

00:05:27.669 --> 00:05:32.139
Combine this with Papa Powell’s money printer,
and it didn’t take long for stocks to recover,

00:05:32.139 --> 00:05:35.090
and this time tens of millions of retail traders
were on board.

00:05:35.090 --> 00:05:39.930
It looked like things couldn’t get any better
for Robinhood, but in the background, there

00:05:39.930 --> 00:05:42.129
were actually several red flags.

00:05:42.129 --> 00:05:47.569
BROKEN REVENUE MODEL:
Likely the biggest red flag surrounding Robinhood

00:05:47.569 --> 00:05:48.669
was their revenue model.

00:05:48.669 --> 00:05:52.689
While they weren’t directly charging users
for trades, they were charging users from

00:05:52.689 --> 00:05:55.599
the backend through something called payment
for order flow.

00:05:55.599 --> 00:05:59.319
Similar to Google and Facebook collecting
information on our browsing habits and selling

00:05:59.319 --> 00:06:03.279
the data to advertisers, Robinhood was collecting
all of our trades and selling them to wall

00:06:03.279 --> 00:06:05.199
street firms called market makers.

00:06:05.199 --> 00:06:07.979
Now, Robinhood didn’t actually invent this
revenue model.

00:06:07.979 --> 00:06:12.349
Other low costs brokerages like E-Trade have
been leveraging payment for order flow for

00:06:12.349 --> 00:06:15.669
years, but this doesn’t mean it’s a good
strategy.

00:06:15.669 --> 00:06:19.900
Payment for order flow is not only heavily
frowned upon, but actually illegal in several

00:06:19.900 --> 00:06:22.520
countries including the UK, Canada, and Australia.

00:06:22.520 --> 00:06:27.949
But, wait a minute, what’s the problem with
payment for order flow in the first place?

00:06:27.949 --> 00:06:30.659
It gives us basically unlimited free trades.

00:06:30.659 --> 00:06:35.090
Well, the truth is, it’s not actually free,
you’re just charged indirectly.

00:06:35.090 --> 00:06:39.309
You see, market makers aren’t idiots to
pay for our emotional day trades and hype

00:06:39.309 --> 00:06:40.309
investments.

00:06:40.309 --> 00:06:43.530
They’re paying for the order flow in order
to flip our orders and make a profit.

00:06:43.530 --> 00:06:47.569
For example, let’s say you’re willing
to buy 1 share of Apple for $100.

00:06:47.569 --> 00:06:51.150
If this order executes, that means that the
market maker was able to find someone that

00:06:51.150 --> 00:06:55.800
was willing to sell 1 share of Apple for $99.50
or even $99.

00:06:55.800 --> 00:06:59.530
This gives the market maker 50 cents to $1
worth of profit which they’ll split with

00:06:59.530 --> 00:07:00.530
Robinhood.

00:07:00.530 --> 00:07:03.449
The difference between how much you’re willing
to pay and how much the seller is willing

00:07:03.449 --> 00:07:07.440
to sell for is called the bid-ask spread,
and market makers have been leveraging such

00:07:07.440 --> 00:07:09.169
disparities for decades.

00:07:09.169 --> 00:07:13.360
Usually, this isn’t a problem because market
makers only made a penny or two from each

00:07:13.360 --> 00:07:14.360
share.

00:07:14.360 --> 00:07:17.569
But given that market makers working with
Robinhood have to split their profits with

00:07:17.569 --> 00:07:20.969
Robinhood, their bid-ask spreads are generally
way larger.

00:07:20.969 --> 00:07:24.759
In fact, a Wall Street Journal study found
that Robinhood’s spreads were as much as

00:07:24.759 --> 00:07:26.610
60 times larger than Charles Schwab.

00:07:26.610 --> 00:07:31.089
So, with Robinhood, sure you don’t have
to pay 50 cents for every share you purchase

00:07:31.089 --> 00:07:32.089
upfront.

00:07:32.089 --> 00:07:36.039
But, you’re constantly overpaying for every
share you purchase which indirectly charges

00:07:36.039 --> 00:07:38.439
you around the same amount if not more.

00:07:38.439 --> 00:07:41.990
And to make things worse, with traditional
brokerages, since you’re paying the fee,

00:07:41.990 --> 00:07:45.300
you’re the customer and you’re the top
priority for the brokerage.

00:07:45.300 --> 00:07:48.789
But, with Robinhood, since the market makers
are the ones that are technically paying the

00:07:48.789 --> 00:07:53.369
fees even though you’re being charged for
it, market makers are the customer and they’re

00:07:53.369 --> 00:07:54.589
the top priority.

00:07:54.589 --> 00:08:00.110
MORE RED FLAGS:
Robinhood’s broken revenue model would eventually

00:08:00.110 --> 00:08:04.719
come back to haunt them, but in the late 2010s,
they had much bigger issues to address.

00:08:04.719 --> 00:08:09.319
In July of 2019, for example, Robinhood admitted
that they were storing their user’s passwords

00:08:09.319 --> 00:08:13.789
in a readable form across their servers which
had made it easy for hackers to steal passwords

00:08:13.789 --> 00:08:16.389
and subsequently steal money from these accounts.

00:08:16.389 --> 00:08:20.469
Similarly, just a few months ago, Robinhood
confirmed that a voice phishing scheme allowed

00:08:20.469 --> 00:08:25.059
hackers to steal the email addresses of 5
million customers and the full names of 2

00:08:25.059 --> 00:08:26.059
million customers.

00:08:26.059 --> 00:08:29.719
Aside from these security breaches, a couple
of WallStreetBets users had found a glitch

00:08:29.719 --> 00:08:33.159
within Robinhood that allowed them to borrow
infinite amounts of money.

00:08:33.159 --> 00:08:38.360
The glitch entailed purchasing shares on margin
and then selling covered calls on these shares.

00:08:38.360 --> 00:08:42.039
Users could then use the proceeds from selling
covered calls to purchase more stock and sell

00:08:42.039 --> 00:08:45.820
more covered calls on these newly purchased
shares and the cycle continues.

00:08:45.820 --> 00:08:49.639
Many WallStreetBets users used this glitch
to engage in extremely risky trading which

00:08:49.639 --> 00:08:52.680
resulted in many accounts sustaining 6 figure
losses.

00:08:52.680 --> 00:08:56.779
And when all the fun was over, Robinhood was
the one that had to pay the bill.

00:08:56.779 --> 00:09:00.240
But none of these issues holds a candle to
the PR nightmare that followed the suicide

00:09:00.240 --> 00:09:01.880
of Alexander Kearns.

00:09:01.880 --> 00:09:06.440
Alexander was a University of Nebraska student
that had been margin trading on Robinhood.

00:09:06.440 --> 00:09:10.279
This wasn’t going too well though and at
one point, Robinhood said that his account

00:09:10.279 --> 00:09:13.500
balance was -$730,000.

00:09:13.500 --> 00:09:17.860
After seeing this, Alexander took his life,
and on his suicide note, he accused Robinhood

00:09:17.860 --> 00:09:20.230
of allowing him to take on too much risk.

00:09:20.230 --> 00:09:25.370
The worst part was that the -$730,000 account
balance wasn’t even legitimate.

00:09:25.370 --> 00:09:29.220
It was a temporary settlement glitch that
would’ve resolved by itself.

00:09:29.220 --> 00:09:33.459
As you would guess, this led to several lawsuits
regarding gamification and risk disclosures

00:09:33.459 --> 00:09:35.709
many of which are still ongoing.

00:09:35.709 --> 00:09:39.490
But all these lawsuits were just a sneak peek
into what would follow after the GameStop

00:09:39.490 --> 00:09:40.589
fiasco.

00:09:40.589 --> 00:09:45.899
ONE LAST HOORAH:
By now, I’m sure all of y’all are familiar

00:09:45.899 --> 00:09:47.910
with what happened to GameStop.

00:09:47.910 --> 00:09:51.329
WallStreetBets identified a massive short
interest in GameStop and proceeded to buy

00:09:51.329 --> 00:09:52.779
heavily into the stock.

00:09:52.779 --> 00:09:56.930
It’s not clear if this actually resulted
in a short squeeze or not, but the hype and

00:09:56.930 --> 00:10:02.110
momentum itself took GameStop to the moon,
and Robinhood was at the center of this movement.

00:10:02.110 --> 00:10:05.459
When things started getting exciting though,
Robinhood restricted users from purchasing

00:10:05.459 --> 00:10:07.889
GameStop and other short squeeze stocks.

00:10:07.889 --> 00:10:11.540
Their official reasoning for this was that
these stocks were getting too volatile and

00:10:11.540 --> 00:10:14.250
that they were trying to protect their users
from massive losses.

00:10:14.250 --> 00:10:18.759
But, given their history with risk, this was
rather hard to believe and suspicions went

00:10:18.759 --> 00:10:19.810
elsewhere.

00:10:19.810 --> 00:10:23.750
One of Robinhood’s biggest market-maker
customers is a company called Citadel which

00:10:23.750 --> 00:10:26.660
also happened to have a major short position
in GameStop.

00:10:26.660 --> 00:10:31.089
Now, this isn’t confirmed, but it seems
like Robinhood was pressured by Citadel to

00:10:31.089 --> 00:10:32.519
halt trading on GameStop.

00:10:32.519 --> 00:10:36.370
And fearful of losing one of their biggest
customers, Robinhood complied.

00:10:36.370 --> 00:10:40.339
See, this is the problem with your customers
and your users being different people.

00:10:40.339 --> 00:10:44.370
This trading halt was followed up by dozens
of class-action lawsuits, but this was the

00:10:44.370 --> 00:10:46.319
least of Robinhood’s problems.

00:10:46.319 --> 00:10:50.639
Whether the speculation around Robinhood’s
actions is true or not, the incident destroyed

00:10:50.639 --> 00:10:55.430
their reputation as the broker of the people
and their user growth and suffered every since.

00:10:55.430 --> 00:10:59.899
Despite this poor outlook, Robinhood would
actually experience one last hoorah accidentally.

00:10:59.899 --> 00:11:06.220
In March, April, and May of 2021, Dogecoin
would rocket from a few cents up to 73 cents,

00:11:06.220 --> 00:11:11.760
and given that you could buy Dogecoin on Robinhood,
this gave Robinhood one more spectacular quarter.

00:11:11.760 --> 00:11:16.069
But after Elon Musk’s appearance on Saturday
Night Live, Dogecoin fell off a cliff and

00:11:16.069 --> 00:11:17.440
so did Robinhood.

00:11:17.440 --> 00:11:21.420
And Robinhood’s initially hyped IPO ended
up being a massive bust.

00:11:21.420 --> 00:11:26.540
IMPOSSIBLE COMPS:
In terms of Robinhood’s short-term future,

00:11:26.540 --> 00:11:28.709
it’s really not looking that great.

00:11:28.709 --> 00:11:33.029
Sentiment surrounding Robinhood is still as
bad as ever and it’s going to be nearly

00:11:33.029 --> 00:11:37.279
impossible to beat their revenue numbers from
last year which largely benefitted from GameStop

00:11:37.279 --> 00:11:38.410
and Dogecoin.

00:11:38.410 --> 00:11:42.660
Fortunately, Robinhood has billions in the
bank and can weather a few painful quarters

00:11:42.660 --> 00:11:44.660
or even a few painful years.

00:11:44.660 --> 00:11:50.160
Maybe Robinhood’s phenomenal UI will end
up shining through to retail investors, but

00:11:50.160 --> 00:11:52.000
we’ll just have to wait and see.

00:11:52.000 --> 00:11:55.629
At the end of the day, while Robinhood did
several things right, it seems like their

00:11:55.629 --> 00:11:57.430
entire mission was a bit misguided.

00:11:57.430 --> 00:12:02.399
You see, I don’t think people hate Wall
Street because of high fees or prices, I think

00:12:02.399 --> 00:12:05.790
people hate wall street due to their lack
of transparency and accountability.

00:12:05.790 --> 00:12:11.019
So, the key wasn’t to open a free brokerage,
but rather to open a transparent and accountable

00:12:11.019 --> 00:12:12.019
brokerage.

00:12:12.019 --> 00:12:15.399
This is one of the main reasons that Vanguard
is so highly respected.

00:12:15.399 --> 00:12:19.610
Vanguard doesn’t have shareholders or customers
that are different from their users.

00:12:19.610 --> 00:12:22.779
So, Vanguard’s interests are always aligned
with their users.

00:12:22.779 --> 00:12:26.920
Porting this business model to mobile devices
paired with a tremendous UI is what I think

00:12:26.920 --> 00:12:31.980
Robinhood should’ve done, but instead, they’ve
become the epitome of Wall Street, the exact

00:12:31.980 --> 00:12:33.829
thing that they were trying to fight.

00:12:33.829 --> 00:12:37.940
Would you prefer to use a brokerage with upfront
fees or a brokerage with backend fees?

00:12:37.940 --> 00:12:39.080
Comment that down below.

00:12:39.080 --> 00:12:43.370
Also, drop a like if you’re glad that Robinhood
got a taste of their own medicine.

00:12:43.370 --> 00:12:47.579
And of course, consider checking out our international
channels to watch our videos in other languages

00:12:47.579 --> 00:12:53.759
